{"title": "Exoenzyme", "kind": "Concept", "weight": "Major", "level": "Intermediate", "article": "Exoenzyme", "definition": {"text": "An enzyme that a cell secretes and that works outside it, most often to break down large molecules.", "sent_ids": ["bda85454be94", "a833c9e03e4a"]}, "facts": [{"text": "Breaking macromolecules into small pieces lets their constituents pass through the cell membrane and be used inside the cell.", "sent_ids": ["de71a242982b", "585dd2d2f667"]}, {"text": "Bacteria and fungi digest nutrients in their surroundings this way; once inside, the nutrients are processed with help from endoenzymes.", "sent_ids": ["47a74553f643", "a5441715e8cc"]}, {"text": "In soil they target carbohydrates (cellulases), lignin (oxidases), organic phosphates (phosphatases), amino sugar polymers (chitinases) and proteins (proteases).", "sent_ids": ["ce03452b6cb9"]}, {"text": "Grouped as hydrolases, lyases, oxidoreductases and transferases, they control soil enzyme activity by degrading biopolymers.", "sent_ids": ["18f653629e60"]}, {"text": "Fungi used in bioremediation secrete oxidative exoenzymes, such as laccases, from their growing hyphal tips.", "sent_ids": ["d434a196bc7b", "cea0d0eff1ce", "d9b90f577f82"]}, {"text": "Cell-free microbial exoenzymes can also clean up pollution, but they are often less robust and hard to introduce into soil.", "sent_ids": ["70acb8586480"]}, {"text": "The word 'exoenzyme' entered English in 1908; pepsin and trypsin were probably the first known.", "sent_ids": ["efc8fc3ece77", "40d387e21303"]}], "prerequisites": ["soil-bio-039", "soil-bio-115"], "related": ["soil-bio-254", "soil-env-023", "soil-bio-125"], "contested": false, "contested_note": null, "conflicts": [], "distinct_from": [], "wants_image": false, "notes": null, "part": "Soil biology", "chapter": "Soil biochemistry", "id": "soil-bio-253", "score": 16.35, "score_parts": {"importance": 2.773, "weight": 3, "tests": 3, "pages": 1.58, "builds_on_it": 6.0}, "selected": true, "verification": [], "external": [], "source_tier": "wikipedia", "curriculum": ["own article", "mentioned on 2+ pages", "prerequisite of 2+"], "sources": [{"title": "Exoenzyme", "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Exoenzyme", "revid": 1374741074, "sent_ids": ["de71a242982b", "585dd2d2f667", "47a74553f643", "a5441715e8cc", "d434a196bc7b", "cea0d0eff1ce", "d9b90f577f82", "70acb8586480", "efc8fc3ece77", "40d387e21303", "bda85454be94", "a833c9e03e4a"]}, {"title": "Fungal extracellular enzyme activity", "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Fungal_extracellular_enzyme_activity", "revid": 1347475252, "sent_ids": ["ce03452b6cb9", "18f653629e60"]}], "events": [{"date_text": "in 1908", "date_ma": 4.2e-05, "sent_ids": ["efc8fc3ece77"]}], "flags": [], "qid": "Q411761", "prerequisites_draft": ["soil-bio-039", "soil-bio-115"], "prerequisite_sources": {"soil-bio-039": "ordering pass", "soil-bio-115": "ordering pass"}, "unit": 226, "position": 1}
{"title": "Fungal extracellular enzyme activity", "kind": "Process", "weight": "Major", "level": "Advanced", "article": "Fungal extracellular enzyme activity", "definition": {"text": "The secretion by fungi of enzymes that break down complex organic matter outside the cell; most such enzymes in leaf litter and soil come from fungi.", "sent_ids": ["322af3e7caf1", "dc6c8f06ffb1"]}, "facts": [{"text": "Making and secreting enzymes is energy-intensive, so evolution favours limiting production.", "sent_ids": ["99b759709b6e"]}, {"text": "Some exoenzymes are made constitutively at low levels and boosted when their substrate is plentiful, while glucose represses cellulase production.", "sent_ids": ["3aa948435c38", "b1966ef4ad48"]}, {"text": "Soil pH, temperature, moisture and litter type and quality alter enzyme expression and activity, and fertilizer and tillage shift it through the profile.", "sent_ids": ["16e3f7a7640e", "43c9d172ff76"]}, {"text": "In decaying litter, cellulose and other labile substrates go first; lignin is depolymerised later as oxidative enzyme activity rises.", "sent_ids": ["5608718d755b"]}, {"text": "Cellulose is cut by hydrolytic endoglucanases, cellobiohydrolase and β-glucosidase; lignin by phenol oxidases, peroxidases and laccases.", "sent_ids": ["fe75f108737e", "910e12bc80d5"]}, {"text": "Lignin peroxidase and manganese peroxidase are the hallmark of basidiomycetes and are used to gauge their activity.", "sent_ids": ["61fa78277bac"]}, {"text": "Enzyme activity serves as an indicator of soil quality.", "sent_ids": ["6165ea70a3c7"]}], "prerequisites": ["soil-bio-253", "soil-bio-003", "soil-bio-116", "soil-bio-106", "soil-bio-039"], "related": ["soil-bio-129", "soil-bio-231", "soil-bio-255", "soil-bio-258", "soil-bio-259", "soil-bio-257"], "contested": false, "contested_note": null, "conflicts": [], "distinct_from": ["Soil enzyme assays"], "wants_image": false, "notes": null, "part": "Soil biology", "chapter": "Soil biochemistry", "id": "soil-bio-254", "score": 8.7, "score_parts": {"importance": 2.197, "weight": 3, "tests": 1, "pages": 1.0, "builds_on_it": 1.5}, "selected": true, "verification": [], "external": [], "source_tier": "wikipedia", "curriculum": ["own article"], "sources": [{"title": "Fungal extracellular enzyme activity", "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Fungal_extracellular_enzyme_activity", "revid": 1347475252, "sent_ids": ["99b759709b6e", "3aa948435c38", "b1966ef4ad48", "16e3f7a7640e", "43c9d172ff76", "5608718d755b", "fe75f108737e", "910e12bc80d5", "61fa78277bac", "6165ea70a3c7", "322af3e7caf1", "dc6c8f06ffb1"]}], "events": [], "flags": [], "qid": "Q17165743", "prerequisites_draft": ["soil-bio-253", "soil-bio-039", "soil-bio-116", "soil-bio-106"], "prerequisite_sources": {"soil-bio-253": "ordering pass", "soil-bio-003": "ordering pass", "soil-bio-116": "ordering pass", "soil-bio-106": "ordering pass", "soil-bio-039": "consolidation"}, "unit": 226, "position": 2}
{"title": "Soil enzyme assays", "kind": "Method", "weight": "Minor", "level": "Advanced", "article": "Fungal extracellular enzyme activity#Measuring fungal extracellular enzyme activity in soil, plant litter, and other environmental samples", "definition": {"text": "Laboratory methods that estimate enzyme activity in soil or litter by mixing samples with buffers and a substrate.", "sent_ids": ["5f24c5abfb57"]}, "facts": [{"text": "Hydrolytic enzymes are measured colorimetrically with p-nitrophenol-linked substrates or fluorometrically with 4-methylumbelliferone-linked ones.", "sent_ids": ["b56958b985f1"]}, {"text": "Phenol oxidase is measured by oxidation of L-DOPA, pyrogallol or ABTS; peroxidase is the difference when the same assay is rerun with hydrogen peroxide added.", "sent_ids": ["261fa90590a2", "4ae05d9bb34a", "084d8deea8ac"]}, {"text": "Results depend on sample transport and storage, assay pH, substrate concentration, temperature and sample preparation.", "sent_ids": ["74ff12d0e36c"]}, {"text": "Assays mostly reveal exo-acting enzymes that cut from chain ends; molecular methods now count enzyme-coding genes to link enzymes with their producers.", "sent_ids": ["88f9c473ef2c", "d862b9173377", "f871d294925d"]}], "prerequisites": ["soil-bio-039", "soil-bio-253", "soil-bio-254"], "related": ["soil-bio-256", "soil-bio-257"], "contested": false, "contested_note": null, "conflicts": [], "distinct_from": ["Fungal extracellular enzyme activity"], "wants_image": false, "notes": null, "mention_sent_ids": ["23fa73a3f3cf", "3329a473df9c"], "part": "Soil biology", "chapter": "Soil biochemistry", "id": "soil-bio-255", "score": 4.2, "score_parts": {"importance": 2.197, "weight": 0, "tests": 1, "pages": 1.0, "builds_on_it": 0.0}, "selected": true, "verification": [], "external": [], "source_tier": "wikipedia", "curriculum": ["own section"], "sources": [{"title": "Fungal extracellular enzyme activity", "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Fungal_extracellular_enzyme_activity", "revid": 1347475252, "sent_ids": ["b56958b985f1", "261fa90590a2", "4ae05d9bb34a", "084d8deea8ac", "74ff12d0e36c", "88f9c473ef2c", "d862b9173377", "f871d294925d", "5f24c5abfb57", "23fa73a3f3cf", "3329a473df9c"]}], "events": [], "flags": [], "qid": null, "prerequisites_draft": ["soil-bio-254", "soil-bio-039"], "prerequisite_sources": {"soil-bio-039": "ordering pass", "soil-bio-253": "ordering pass", "soil-bio-254": "consolidation"}, "unit": 228, "position": 5}
{"title": "Acid phosphatase", "kind": "Material", "weight": "Minor", "level": "Intermediate", "article": "Acid phosphatase", "definition": {"text": "An enzyme that frees attached phosphate groups from other molecules, working best at acidic pH.", "sent_ids": ["f30b6f4f6f26", "ebaac450f278"]}, "facts": [{"text": "It hydrolyses a phosphate monoester with water to give an alcohol and phosphate, at an optimum pH below 7.", "sent_ids": ["ebaac450f278", "3b579283f686"]}, {"text": "Soil microorganisms use phosphatase enzymes to reach organically bound phosphate.", "sent_ids": ["392a952821f9"]}, {"text": "Assays of phosphatase activity can reveal the biological demand for phosphate in a soil.", "sent_ids": ["36356ff2bb57"]}, {"text": "Some roots, especially cluster roots, exude carboxylates with acid phosphatase activity that mobilise phosphorus in nutrient-poor soils.", "sent_ids": ["496208352a51"]}], "prerequisites": ["soil-bio-039", "soil-found-014"], "related": ["soil-bio-191", "soil-bio-255", "soil-bio-253", "soil-fert-127"], "contested": false, "contested_note": null, "conflicts": [], "distinct_from": [], "wants_image": false, "notes": null, "part": "Soil biology", "chapter": "Soil biochemistry", "id": "soil-bio-256", "score": 4.2, "score_parts": {"importance": 2.197, "weight": 0, "tests": 1, "pages": 1.0, "builds_on_it": 0.0}, "selected": true, "verification": [], "external": [], "source_tier": "wikipedia", "curriculum": ["own article"], "sources": [{"title": "Acid phosphatase", "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Acid_phosphatase", "revid": 1376082663, "sent_ids": ["ebaac450f278", "3b579283f686", "392a952821f9", "36356ff2bb57", "496208352a51", "f30b6f4f6f26"]}], "events": [], "flags": [], "qid": "Q418243", "prerequisites_draft": ["soil-bio-039", "soil-fert-127"], "prerequisite_sources": {"soil-bio-039": "ordering pass", "soil-found-014": "ordering pass"}, "unit": 228, "position": 1}
{"title": "Β-Glucosidase", "kind": "Material", "weight": "Minor", "level": "Intermediate", "article": "Β-Glucosidase", "definition": {"text": "An enzyme that hydrolyses terminal β-D-glucosyl residues, releasing glucose.", "sent_ids": ["7c4cacaf0a26", "e5eb05d64aa9"]}, "facts": [{"text": "Together with cellulases (endoglucanases) and cellobiosidases it is needed to consume cellulose, a polymer of β-1,4-linked glucose.", "sent_ids": ["e78aeda95644"]}, {"text": "It finishes cellulose breakdown by splitting cellobiose into glucose; removing cellobiose also relieves its inhibition of other cellulose enzymes.", "sent_ids": ["2f335a5e308f", "c810ff7b601d"]}, {"text": "Both white- and brown-rot fungi secrete it, and it can also cleave xylose, mannose and galactose.", "sent_ids": ["25af9db5c455", "c07bcf6200be"]}, {"text": "Soil and litter assays track cellulose degradation through cellobiohydrolase and β-glucosidase activity on pNP or MUF substrates.", "sent_ids": ["a8aa4211881e"]}], "prerequisites": ["soil-bio-039", "soil-bio-115"], "related": ["soil-bio-254", "soil-bio-255", "soil-bio-232"], "contested": false, "contested_note": null, "conflicts": [], "distinct_from": [], "wants_image": false, "notes": null, "part": "Soil biology", "chapter": "Soil biochemistry", "id": "soil-bio-257", "score": 4.97, "score_parts": {"importance": 1.386, "weight": 0, "tests": 2, "pages": 1.58, "builds_on_it": 0.0}, "selected": true, "verification": [], "external": [], "source_tier": "wikipedia", "curriculum": ["own article", "mentioned on 2+ pages"], "sources": [{"title": "Β-Glucosidase", "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/%CE%92-Glucosidase", "revid": 1385851985, "sent_ids": ["e78aeda95644", "2f335a5e308f", "c810ff7b601d", "7c4cacaf0a26", "e5eb05d64aa9"]}, {"title": "Fungal extracellular enzyme activity", "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Fungal_extracellular_enzyme_activity", "revid": 1347475252, "sent_ids": ["25af9db5c455", "c07bcf6200be", "a8aa4211881e"]}], "events": [], "flags": [], "qid": "Q3266947", "prerequisites_draft": ["soil-bio-039", "soil-bio-115"], "prerequisite_sources": {"soil-bio-039": "ordering pass", "soil-bio-115": "consolidation"}, "unit": 228, "position": 4}
{"title": "Lignin peroxidase", "kind": "Material", "weight": "Minor", "level": "Advanced", "article": "Lignin peroxidase", "definition": {"text": "A heme-containing peroxidase enzyme, one of the ligninases, that uses hydrogen peroxide to cleave lignin-like compounds.", "sent_ids": ["191d417a2207", "29946373e9a2", "7e18770c49eb"]}, "facts": [{"text": "Lignin resists biodegradation so strongly that only higher fungi and some bacteria can break it down, by oxidation.", "sent_ids": ["4b17fdde45c3"]}, {"text": "Fungi such as Phanerochaete chrysosporium produce it to degrade lignin.", "sent_ids": ["464f6ebdd48d"]}, {"text": "It works with veratryl alcohol, a secondary metabolite of white-rot fungi that acts as its cofactor.", "sent_ids": ["c669cb7ddbbb"]}, {"text": "Despite twenty years of study, its mechanism is not yet fully elucidated.", "sent_ids": ["18aabe09b990"]}], "prerequisites": ["soil-bio-116", "soil-bio-253"], "related": ["soil-bio-259", "soil-bio-129", "soil-bio-254"], "contested": false, "contested_note": null, "conflicts": [], "distinct_from": [], "wants_image": false, "notes": null, "part": "Soil biology", "chapter": "Soil biochemistry", "id": "soil-bio-258", "score": 4.77, "score_parts": {"importance": 2.773, "weight": 0, "tests": 1, "pages": 1.0, "builds_on_it": 0.0}, "selected": true, "verification": [], "external": [], "source_tier": "wikipedia", "curriculum": ["own article"], "sources": [{"title": "Lignin peroxidase", "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Lignin_peroxidase", "revid": 1311279992, "sent_ids": ["4b17fdde45c3", "464f6ebdd48d", "c669cb7ddbbb", "18aabe09b990", "191d417a2207", "29946373e9a2", "7e18770c49eb"]}], "events": [], "flags": [], "qid": "Q2292906", "prerequisites_draft": ["soil-bio-116", "soil-bio-253"], "prerequisite_sources": {"soil-bio-116": "ordering pass", "soil-bio-253": "ordering pass"}, "unit": 227, "position": 1}
{"title": "Manganese peroxidase", "kind": "Material", "weight": "Minor", "level": "Advanced", "article": "Manganese peroxidase", "definition": {"text": "A heme peroxidase enzyme that uses hydrogen peroxide to oxidise manganese(II) to manganese(III).", "sent_ids": ["e17c784a98a1", "19f0b8c62df2", "14bb89c1703c"]}, "facts": [{"text": "White-rot fungi secrete it to degrade lignin; discovered in Phanerochaete chrysosporium in 1985, it seems unique to Basidiomycota.", "sent_ids": ["f38007fd2d0d", "10d3252dbf29", "19afb0f59e56"]}, {"text": "Unstable Mn(III) is released as a chelate with a carboxylic acid, most commonly oxalate.", "sent_ids": ["1f751a4a2448", "a9f124c8f9c5"]}, {"text": "The Mn(III) oxidises phenolic compounds in lignin directly and can cleave alkyl–aryl bonds.", "sent_ids": ["63984178e087", "8f3aeb003084"]}, {"text": "Rising extracellular Mn(II) and hydrogen peroxide up-regulate it, and lignin also induces it.", "sent_ids": ["8c723bccbf32", "51d363ccc7d4"]}], "prerequisites": ["soil-bio-116", "soil-bio-253", "soil-chem-178"], "related": ["soil-bio-258", "soil-bio-129", "soil-bio-254"], "contested": false, "contested_note": null, "conflicts": [], "distinct_from": [], "wants_image": false, "notes": null, "part": "Soil biology", "chapter": "Soil biochemistry", "id": "soil-bio-259", "score": 5.78, "score_parts": {"importance": 2.197, "weight": 0, "tests": 2, "pages": 1.58, "builds_on_it": 0.0}, "selected": true, "verification": [], "external": [], "source_tier": "wikipedia", "curriculum": ["own article", "mentioned on 2+ pages"], "sources": [{"title": "Manganese peroxidase", "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Manganese_peroxidase", "revid": 1298737999, "sent_ids": ["f38007fd2d0d", "10d3252dbf29", "19afb0f59e56", "1f751a4a2448", "a9f124c8f9c5", "63984178e087", "8f3aeb003084", "8c723bccbf32", "e17c784a98a1", "19f0b8c62df2", "14bb89c1703c"]}, {"title": "Fungal extracellular enzyme activity", "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Fungal_extracellular_enzyme_activity", "revid": 1347475252, "sent_ids": ["51d363ccc7d4"]}], "events": [{"date_text": "in 1985", "date_ma": -3.5e-05, "sent_ids": ["10d3252dbf29"]}], "flags": [], "qid": "Q2292892", "prerequisites_draft": ["soil-bio-116", "soil-bio-253"], "prerequisite_sources": {"soil-bio-116": "ordering pass", "soil-bio-253": "ordering pass", "soil-chem-178": "ordering pass"}, "unit": 227, "position": 3}
{"title": "Amino sugar", "kind": "Material", "weight": "Minor", "level": "Intermediate", "article": "Amino sugar", "definition": {"text": "A sugar molecule in which a hydroxyl group has been replaced by an amine group.", "sent_ids": ["9b7e57776936"]}, "facts": [{"text": "More than 60 are known; one of the most abundant, N-acetyl-D-glucosamine, is the main component of chitin.", "sent_ids": ["a510f07ffd8c"]}, {"text": "Soil microbes break down amino sugar polymers with chitinases.", "sent_ids": ["ce03452b6cb9"]}, {"text": "Decomposing plant material yields simpler forms, including sugars and amino sugars, that become microbial biomass or humic assemblages.", "sent_ids": ["f02003db3d7f"]}], "prerequisites": ["soil-bio-115"], "related": ["soil-bio-253", "soil-bio-090"], "contested": false, "contested_note": null, "conflicts": [], "distinct_from": [], "wants_image": false, "notes": null, "part": "Soil biology", "chapter": "Soil biochemistry", "id": "soil-bio-260", "score": 6.2, "score_parts": {"importance": 2.197, "weight": 0, "tests": 2, "pages": 2.0, "builds_on_it": 0.0}, "selected": true, "verification": [], "external": [], "source_tier": "wikipedia", "curriculum": ["own article", "mentioned on 2+ pages"], "sources": [{"title": "Amino sugar", "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Amino_sugar", "revid": 1335513690, "sent_ids": ["a510f07ffd8c", "9b7e57776936"]}, {"title": "Fungal extracellular enzyme activity", "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Fungal_extracellular_enzyme_activity", "revid": 1347475252, "sent_ids": ["ce03452b6cb9"]}, {"title": "Humus", "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Humus", "revid": 1378637328, "sent_ids": ["f02003db3d7f"]}], "events": [], "flags": [], "qid": "Q414083", "prerequisites_draft": ["soil-bio-115"], "prerequisite_sources": {"soil-bio-115": "consolidation"}, "unit": 227, "position": 4}
{"title": "Amplicon", "kind": "Concept", "weight": "Minor", "level": "Advanced", "article": "Amplicon", "definition": {"text": "A piece of DNA that is the source or product of amplification or replication.", "sent_ids": ["2adb5dbbfca2"]}, "facts": [{"text": "Amplicons are made artificially by the polymerase or ligase chain reaction, so the term is used interchangeably with 'PCR product'.", "sent_ids": ["c7a37346e190", "576be0673d60"]}, {"text": "Bacteria and archaea can be classified by comparing amplicons of the highly conserved 16S rRNA gene with known sequences.", "sent_ids": ["69d3e03b1f72"]}, {"text": "Fungi are classified the same way from the 18S rRNA gene and the ITS1 region.", "sent_ids": ["36c30f402537"]}, {"text": "In amplicon sequencing, counts of identical taxa give their relative abundance in the sample.", "sent_ids": ["ab7097cbdf70", "f32be128bb50"]}], "prerequisites": ["soil-bio-011"], "related": ["soil-bio-262", "soil-bio-263"], "contested": false, "contested_note": null, "conflicts": [], "distinct_from": [], "wants_image": false, "notes": null, "part": "Soil biology", "chapter": "Soil biochemistry", "id": "soil-bio-261", "score": 9.22, "score_parts": {"importance": 3.219, "weight": 0, "tests": 2, "pages": 1.0, "builds_on_it": 3.0}, "selected": true, "verification": [], "external": [], "source_tier": "wikipedia", "curriculum": ["own article", "prerequisite of 2+"], "sources": [{"title": "Amplicon", "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Amplicon", "revid": 1380235070, "sent_ids": ["c7a37346e190", "576be0673d60", "69d3e03b1f72", "36c30f402537", "ab7097cbdf70", "f32be128bb50", "2adb5dbbfca2"]}], "events": [], "flags": [], "qid": "Q2844457", "prerequisites_draft": ["soil-bio-011"], "prerequisite_sources": {"soil-bio-011": "ordering pass"}, "unit": 226, "position": 3}
{"title": "Metabarcoding", "kind": "Method", "weight": "Minor", "level": "Advanced", "article": "Metabarcoding", "definition": {"text": "DNA or RNA barcoding done so that many taxa in one sample are identified at once.", "sent_ids": ["a7329d307779"]}, "facts": [{"text": "Unlike barcoding, it aims at the species composition of a sample, passing through DNA extraction, PCR amplification, sequencing and data analysis.", "sent_ids": ["5b4e59ff5be2", "32efccd85f1a"]}, {"text": "Soil can be a bulk sample of collected organisms or a source of environmental DNA taken without separating the organisms.", "sent_ids": ["5e26cfd62ee0", "1a9e6c6e34af"]}, {"text": "Soil contains inhibitors, so special techniques are needed to isolate its DNA.", "sent_ids": ["9427fe50ea62"]}, {"text": "There is not yet consensus on the best experimental design and bioinformatics criteria.", "sent_ids": ["22362a7b1da9"]}], "prerequisites": ["soil-bio-261", "soil-bio-011"], "related": ["soil-bio-263", "soil-bio-025"], "contested": false, "contested_note": null, "conflicts": [], "distinct_from": [], "wants_image": false, "notes": null, "part": "Soil biology", "chapter": "Soil biochemistry", "id": "soil-bio-262", "score": 4.77, "score_parts": {"importance": 2.773, "weight": 0, "tests": 1, "pages": 1.0, "builds_on_it": 0.0}, "selected": true, "verification": [], "external": [], "source_tier": "wikipedia", "curriculum": ["own article"], "sources": [{"title": "Metabarcoding", "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Metabarcoding", "revid": 1384030135, "sent_ids": ["5b4e59ff5be2", "32efccd85f1a", "5e26cfd62ee0", "1a9e6c6e34af", "9427fe50ea62", "22362a7b1da9", "a7329d307779"]}], "events": [], "flags": [], "qid": "Q51237016", "prerequisites_draft": ["soil-bio-261", "soil-bio-011"], "prerequisite_sources": {"soil-bio-261": "ordering pass", "soil-bio-011": "ordering pass"}, "unit": 226, "position": 4}
{"title": "Stable-isotope probing", "kind": "Method", "weight": "Minor", "level": "Advanced", "article": "Stable-isotope probing", "definition": {"text": "A microbial ecology technique that traces nutrient uptake by microorganisms by feeding them substrate enriched in a heavy stable isotope.", "sent_ids": ["3924c02a0922", "639b36fc5fe3"]}, "facts": [{"text": "Biomarkers carrying the heavy isotope are separated from unlabelled ones by isopycnic centrifugation.", "sent_ids": ["a9bece7fc36f"]}, {"text": "With 13CO2, labelled DNA is spun out and sequenced to show which organisms use freshly made photosynthate; 18O-water shows which are actively growing.", "sent_ids": ["95773e717e6e", "10fe3e595177", "48befed734c0", "608995fbe1a1"]}, {"text": "13C is used most often; 15N gives a weaker DNA density shift because DNA holds fewer nitrogen atoms.", "sent_ids": ["b0cc3dbcde6c", "1696ac6aa990"]}, {"text": "Because the density shift is predictable, quantitative SIP (qSIP) can estimate how much isotope was incorporated.", "sent_ids": ["927221fd3e1f"]}], "prerequisites": ["soil-bio-011", "soil-bio-219", "soil-bio-261"], "related": ["soil-bio-262", "soil-bio-269"], "contested": false, "contested_note": null, "conflicts": [], "distinct_from": [], "wants_image": false, "notes": null, "part": "Soil biology", "chapter": "Soil biochemistry", "id": "soil-bio-263", "score": 2.0, "score_parts": {"importance": 0.0, "weight": 0, "tests": 1, "pages": 1.0, "builds_on_it": 0.0}, "selected": true, "verification": [], "external": [], "source_tier": "wikipedia", "curriculum": ["own article"], "sources": [{"title": "Stable-isotope probing", "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Stable-isotope_probing", "revid": 1372906579, "sent_ids": ["a9bece7fc36f", "95773e717e6e", "10fe3e595177", "48befed734c0", "608995fbe1a1", "b0cc3dbcde6c", "1696ac6aa990", "927221fd3e1f", "3924c02a0922", "639b36fc5fe3"]}], "events": [], "flags": [], "qid": "Q17096563", "prerequisites_draft": ["soil-bio-011", "soil-bio-261"], "prerequisite_sources": {"soil-bio-011": "ordering pass", "soil-bio-219": "ordering pass", "soil-bio-261": "consolidation"}, "unit": 229, "position": 3}
{"title": "Ecological stoichiometry", "kind": "Concept", "weight": "Major", "level": "Advanced", "article": "Ecological stoichiometry", "definition": {"text": "The study of how the balance of energy and elements affects living systems, and how organisms affect that balance.", "sent_ids": ["13ec085b0564", "7b4c2d1081e5"]}, "facts": [{"text": "Like chemical stoichiometry it rests on mass balance, an idea traced to Liebig, Lotka and Redfield.", "sent_ids": ["a7042d353526", "1687792f660c"]}, {"text": "A mismatch between what organisms need and what their food holds is an elemental imbalance: termites have tissue C:N of about 5 but eat wood of 300–1000.", "sent_ids": ["dc3ece292297", "5992c8abefea"]}, {"text": "Plants have weak stoichiometric homeostasis and vary widely in composition, while animals keep close to a fixed composition.", "sent_ids": ["0856fc5f60c0", "6d7e9e5019dc", "736188faf6f7"]}, {"text": "It has been applied to nutrient recycling, resource competition, animal growth and nutrient limitation in whole ecosystems.", "sent_ids": ["7e7b83aa5ebe"]}, {"text": "A 2007 study found global average C:N:P ratios of 186:13:1 in soil and 60:7:1 in soil microbial biomass.", "sent_ids": ["d0a4eeecda36"]}, {"text": "The growth rate hypothesis holds that phosphorus demand rises during active growth, to build P-rich nucleic acids.", "sent_ids": ["4e5bb5476493"]}], "prerequisites": [], "related": ["soil-bio-269", "soil-bio-120", "soil-fert-121", "soil-bio-127", "soil-fert-099", "soil-fert-127", "soil-fert-184"], "contested": false, "contested_note": null, "conflicts": [], "distinct_from": [], "wants_image": false, "notes": null, "part": "Soil biology", "chapter": "Soil biochemistry", "id": "soil-bio-264", "score": 6.58, "score_parts": {"importance": 0.0, "weight": 3, "tests": 2, "pages": 1.58, "builds_on_it": 0.0}, "selected": true, "verification": [], "external": [], "source_tier": "wikipedia", "curriculum": ["own article", "mentioned on 2+ pages"], "sources": [{"title": "Ecological stoichiometry", "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ecological_stoichiometry", "revid": 1313904977, "sent_ids": ["a7042d353526", "1687792f660c", "dc3ece292297", "5992c8abefea", "0856fc5f60c0", "6d7e9e5019dc", "736188faf6f7", "7e7b83aa5ebe", "4e5bb5476493", "13ec085b0564", "7b4c2d1081e5"]}, {"title": "Redfield ratio", "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Redfield_ratio", "revid": 1379975157, "sent_ids": ["d0a4eeecda36"]}], "events": [{"date_text": "from 2007", "date_ma": -5.6999999999999996e-05, "sent_ids": ["d0a4eeecda36"]}], "flags": [], "qid": "Q5333258", "prerequisites_draft": ["soil-fert-184", "soil-fert-099", "soil-fert-127"], "prerequisite_sources": {}, "unit": 228, "position": 3}
{"title": "Michaelis–Menten kinetics", "kind": "Concept", "weight": "Minor", "level": "Advanced", "article": "Michaelis–Menten kinetics", "definition": {"text": "The simplest model of enzyme kinetics, for reactions that turn one substrate into one product.", "sent_ids": ["15f1d654039c"]}, "facts": [{"text": "Its equation gives the rate as v = Va/(Km + a), where V (Vmax) is the limiting rate at saturating substrate concentration.", "sent_ids": ["757a2c867513", "0b739a5f87b2"]}, {"text": "The Michaelis constant Km is the substrate concentration at which the rate is half of V.", "sent_ids": ["5b33e4472e37"]}, {"text": "Michaelis and Menten published the theory in 1913, building on Victor Henri's 1902 equation.", "sent_ids": ["9fa917f35ae0"]}, {"text": "Copiotrophic microbes have a higher Michaelis–Menten constant than oligotrophs.", "sent_ids": ["8e71dd4c7277"]}], "prerequisites": ["soil-bio-039", "soil-bio-253"], "related": ["soil-bio-088", "soil-bio-060", "soil-bio-267"], "contested": false, "contested_note": null, "conflicts": [], "distinct_from": [], "wants_image": false, "notes": null, "part": "Soil biology", "chapter": "Soil biochemistry", "id": "soil-bio-265", "score": 6.35, "score_parts": {"importance": 2.773, "weight": 0, "tests": 2, "pages": 1.58, "builds_on_it": 0.0}, "selected": true, "verification": [], "external": [], "source_tier": "wikipedia", "curriculum": ["own article", "mentioned on 2+ pages"], "sources": [{"title": "Michaelis–Menten kinetics", "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Michaelis%E2%80%93Menten_kinetics", "revid": 1378654706, "sent_ids": ["757a2c867513", "0b739a5f87b2", "5b33e4472e37", "9fa917f35ae0", "15f1d654039c"]}, {"title": "Copiotroph", "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Copiotroph", "revid": 1332556270, "sent_ids": ["8e71dd4c7277"]}], "events": [{"date_text": "In 1913", "date_ma": 3.7e-05, "sent_ids": ["9fa917f35ae0"]}], "flags": [], "qid": "Q613955", "prerequisites_draft": ["soil-bio-039", "soil-bio-253"], "prerequisite_sources": {"soil-bio-039": "ordering pass", "soil-bio-253": "consolidation"}, "unit": 227, "position": 2}
{"title": "Microbial carbon pump", "kind": "Concept", "weight": "Minor", "level": "Advanced", "article": "Microbial carbon pump", "definition": {"text": "A process in which microorganisms turn dissolved organic carbon into refractory forms that resist further microbial degradation.", "sent_ids": ["fde394be9605"]}, "facts": [{"text": "In the ocean it can lock carbon away for centuries to millennia, unlike the biological pump, which relies on sinking particles.", "sent_ids": ["b68f2f07fc4d", "b04152cdc97a"]}, {"text": "A conceptual model of the same name describes how soil microorganisms transform and stabilise soil organic matter.", "sent_ids": ["fb3ba55783d8"]}, {"text": "In it, microbial carbon is either respired to the atmosphere or enters the stable pool as microbial necromass.", "sent_ids": ["23f77870ed1e"]}, {"text": "Under ocean warming, extra carbon is converted to CO2 rather than used to build new microbial biomass.", "sent_ids": ["c56e0afeb614"]}], "prerequisites": ["soil-bio-128", "soil-bio-011", "soil-bio-015"], "related": ["soil-bio-230", "soil-bio-270", "soil-env-106"], "contested": false, "contested_note": null, "conflicts": [], "distinct_from": [], "wants_image": false, "notes": null, "part": "Soil biology", "chapter": "Soil biochemistry", "id": "soil-bio-266", "score": 3.58, "score_parts": {"importance": 0.0, "weight": 0, "tests": 2, "pages": 1.58, "builds_on_it": 0.0}, "selected": true, "verification": [], "external": [], "source_tier": "wikipedia", "curriculum": ["own article", "mentioned on 2+ pages"], "sources": [{"title": "Microbial carbon pump", "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Microbial_carbon_pump", "revid": 1385998445, "sent_ids": ["b68f2f07fc4d", "b04152cdc97a", "c56e0afeb614", "fde394be9605"]}, {"title": "Microbial loop", "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Microbial_loop", "revid": 1369618125, "sent_ids": ["fb3ba55783d8", "23f77870ed1e"]}], "events": [], "flags": [], "qid": "Q137439925", "prerequisites_draft": ["soil-bio-128", "soil-bio-015"], "prerequisite_sources": {"soil-bio-128": "ordering pass", "soil-bio-011": "ordering pass", "soil-bio-015": "consolidation"}, "unit": 229, "position": 2}
{"title": "Q10 (temperature coefficient)", "kind": "Concept", "weight": "Minor", "level": "Advanced", "article": "Q10 (temperature coefficient)", "definition": {"text": "A unitless measure of temperature sensitivity: the factor by which a reaction rate changes for a 10 °C rise.", "sent_ids": ["53b08425b590", "148423e501c1", "e1c054b8da33"]}, "facts": [{"text": "It assumes rates depend exponentially on temperature, with Q10 = (R2/R1)^(10 °C/(T2−T1)), and applies to processes such as respiration and photosynthesis.", "sent_ids": ["c98ce62be5fa", "01456085bbb1", "53b08425b590"]}, {"text": "For most biological systems Q10 is about 2 to 3: rates double or triple with every 10 °C.", "sent_ids": ["ad5c72293d75"]}, {"text": "Environmental scientists often take 0 °C or 10 °C as the reference temperature.", "sent_ids": ["eef3c11b5cc9"]}, {"text": "The idea is thought to come from van 't Hoff's work in the late 19th century.", "sent_ids": ["6ae9609fc7db"]}], "prerequisites": ["soil-bio-033"], "related": ["soil-bio-265", "soil-bio-035"], "contested": false, "contested_note": null, "conflicts": [], "distinct_from": [], "wants_image": false, "notes": null, "part": "Soil biology", "chapter": "Soil biochemistry", "id": "soil-bio-267", "score": 2.0, "score_parts": {"importance": 0.0, "weight": 0, "tests": 1, "pages": 1.0, "builds_on_it": 0.0}, "selected": true, "verification": [], "external": [], "source_tier": "wikipedia", "curriculum": ["own article"], "sources": [{"title": "Q10 (temperature coefficient)", "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Q10_(temperature_coefficient)", "revid": 1383894411, "sent_ids": ["c98ce62be5fa", "01456085bbb1", "53b08425b590", "ad5c72293d75", "eef3c11b5cc9", "6ae9609fc7db", "148423e501c1", "e1c054b8da33"]}], "events": [], "flags": [], "qid": "Q129609", "prerequisites_draft": ["soil-bio-033"], "prerequisite_sources": {"soil-bio-033": "consolidation"}, "unit": 229, "position": 4}
{"title": "Resistome", "kind": "Concept", "weight": "Minor", "level": "Advanced", "article": "Resistome", "definition": {"text": "All the antibiotic resistance genes in communities of pathogenic and non-pathogenic bacteria.", "sent_ids": ["f3ee45ff2ddf"]}, "facts": [{"text": "Soil metagenomes from urban, agricultural and forest sites showed spore-forming soil bacteria resistant to most major antibiotics.", "sent_ids": ["329a22fa579b"]}, {"text": "In soil, resistance genes sit on mobile genetic elements, making soil and water communities reservoirs from which resistance can pass to pathogens.", "sent_ids": ["5c15121773d0", "067003e99f53"]}, {"text": "Sequencing has shown resistance genes exchanged between soil bacteria and clinical pathogens, some matching exactly across five antibiotic classes.", "sent_ids": ["e08df41e3c2b", "648cc4ca9c92"]}, {"text": "Sequences from ancient permafrost show antibiotic resistance existed millennia before human-made antibiotics.", "sent_ids": ["593e97190434"]}], "prerequisites": ["soil-bio-002", "soil-bio-011"], "related": ["soil-bio-262", "soil-bio-261"], "contested": false, "contested_note": null, "conflicts": [], "distinct_from": [], "wants_image": false, "notes": null, "part": "Soil biology", "chapter": "Soil biochemistry", "id": "soil-bio-268", "score": 2.0, "score_parts": {"importance": 0.0, "weight": 0, "tests": 1, "pages": 1.0, "builds_on_it": 0.0}, "selected": true, "verification": [], "external": [], "source_tier": "wikipedia", "curriculum": ["own article"], "sources": [{"title": "Resistome", "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Resistome", "revid": 1341615581, "sent_ids": ["329a22fa579b", "5c15121773d0", "067003e99f53", "e08df41e3c2b", "648cc4ca9c92", "593e97190434", "f3ee45ff2ddf"]}], "events": [], "flags": [], "qid": "Q3457855", "prerequisites_draft": ["soil-bio-011"], "prerequisite_sources": {"soil-bio-002": "ordering pass", "soil-bio-011": "consolidation"}, "unit": 229, "position": 5}
{"title": "Microbial biomass", "kind": "Material", "weight": "Major", "level": "Intermediate", "article": null, "definition": {"text": "The living microbes of soil and their metabolites, a few percent of soil organic matter with a short residence time.", "sent_ids": ["aa7384447cb4"]}, "facts": [{"text": "Temperate grassland soils have held 1–2 t/ha of bacterial and 2–5 t/ha of fungal biomass.", "sent_ids": ["7f008c228e6d"]}, {"text": "Microbial biomass can be estimated by weighing soil before and after fumigation.", "sent_ids": ["021cc4f0a3e9"]}, {"text": "Microbes compete with plants for inorganic nitrogen, phosphorus and potassium, tying them up in their biomass: immobilization.", "sent_ids": ["0bd2d2ab6bc6"]}, {"text": "Microbial biomass is a reservoir of stored soil carbon, one way soil microbes sequester carbon.", "sent_ids": ["00ea84e98e9b", "3e33adef39c1"]}, {"text": "Soil microbial biomass averages a C:N:P ratio of 60:7:1 worldwide.", "sent_ids": ["d0a4eeecda36"]}, {"text": "Tillage can immediately cut microbial biomass carbon and nitrogen, while cover crops raised microbial biomass carbon by roughly 20% in a meta-analysis.", "sent_ids": ["2306ebb9389a", "2d16eb3785ee"]}, {"text": "Microbial biomass and activity are standard measures of soil health.", "sent_ids": ["1bd11d12efe2"]}], "prerequisites": ["soil-bio-011", "soil-found-045"], "related": ["soil-fert-121", "soil-bio-230", "soil-bio-264", "soil-env-174", "soil-bio-270"], "contested": false, "contested_note": null, "conflicts": [], "distinct_from": [], "wants_image": false, "notes": null, "part": "Soil biology", "chapter": "Soil biochemistry", "id": "soil-bio-269", "score": 8.82, "score_parts": {"importance": 0.0, "weight": 3, "tests": 1, "pages": 3.32, "builds_on_it": 1.5}, "selected": true, "verification": [], "external": [], "source_tier": "wikipedia", "curriculum": ["mentioned on 2+ pages"], "sources": [{"title": "Biological carbon fixation", "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Biological_carbon_fixation", "revid": 1368835897, "sent_ids": ["00ea84e98e9b", "3e33adef39c1"]}, {"title": "Agricultural microbiology", "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Agricultural_microbiology", "revid": 1372991300, "sent_ids": ["2306ebb9389a"]}, {"title": "Cover crop", "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cover_crop", "revid": 1383370005, "sent_ids": ["2d16eb3785ee"]}, {"title": "Redfield ratio", "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Redfield_ratio", "revid": 1379975157, "sent_ids": ["d0a4eeecda36"]}, {"title": "Soil", "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Soil", "revid": 1385366388, "sent_ids": ["aa7384447cb4"]}, {"title": "Soil fertility", "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Soil_fertility", "revid": 1374806131, "sent_ids": ["0bd2d2ab6bc6"]}, {"title": "Soil food web", "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Soil_food_web", "revid": 1376254873, "sent_ids": ["021cc4f0a3e9"]}, {"title": "Soil health", "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Soil_health", "revid": 1359878312, "sent_ids": ["7f008c228e6d"]}, {"title": "Urban agriculture", "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Urban_agriculture", "revid": 1386012720, "sent_ids": ["1bd11d12efe2"]}], "events": [{"date_text": "from 2007", "date_ma": -5.6999999999999996e-05, "sent_ids": ["d0a4eeecda36"]}], "flags": [], "qid": null, "prerequisites_draft": ["soil-bio-011", "soil-found-045"], "prerequisite_sources": {"soil-bio-011": "ordering pass", "soil-found-045": "ordering pass"}, "unit": 228, "position": 2}
{"title": "Microbial carbon use efficiency", "kind": "Concept", "weight": "Minor", "level": "Advanced", "article": null, "definition": {"text": "A measure of how much of the carbon a microbe takes up goes into biomass rather than into maintenance energy.", "sent_ids": ["b05da3565491"]}, "facts": [{"text": "Copiotrophs tend to have lower carbon use efficiency than oligotrophs, so they make less protein per gram of oxygen consumed.", "sent_ids": ["d081d93b4986", "69184a968cd3"]}, {"text": "In soil, the balance between catabolism that releases carbon as CO2 and anabolism that builds stable carbon compounds controls net carbon retention.", "sent_ids": ["07a0ea72b958"]}], "prerequisites": ["soil-bio-269", "soil-bio-033", "soil-bio-088"], "related": ["soil-bio-266", "soil-bio-033", "soil-bio-230"], "contested": false, "contested_note": null, "conflicts": [], "distinct_from": [], "wants_image": false, "notes": null, "part": "Soil biology", "chapter": "Soil biochemistry", "id": "soil-bio-270", "score": 2.58, "score_parts": {"importance": 0.0, "weight": 0, "tests": 1, "pages": 1.58, "builds_on_it": 0.0}, "selected": true, "verification": [], "external": [], "source_tier": "wikipedia", "curriculum": ["mentioned on 2+ pages"], "sources": [{"title": "Copiotroph", "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Copiotroph", "revid": 1332556270, "sent_ids": ["d081d93b4986", "69184a968cd3", "b05da3565491"]}, {"title": "Microbial loop", "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Microbial_loop", "revid": 1369618125, "sent_ids": ["07a0ea72b958"]}], "events": [], "flags": [], "qid": null, "prerequisites_draft": ["soil-bio-269", "soil-bio-088"], "prerequisite_sources": {"soil-bio-269": "ordering pass", "soil-bio-033": "ordering pass", "soil-bio-088": "consolidation"}, "unit": 229, "position": 1}
